        {
          "type": "title",
          "text": "On the Convergence Properties of Adaptive Gradient Methods in Non-Convex Optimization",
          "confidence": 0.99,
          "engine": "pdf"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "We analyze the convergence behavior of adaptive gradient methods, including Adam, RMSProp, and AdaGrad, for non-convex optimization problems arising in deep learning. Our theoretical framework provides provable convergence guarantees under mild assumptions on the objective function and stochastic gradients.",
          "confidence": 0.96,
          "engine": "pdf"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Preliminaries",
          "confidence": 0.98,
          "engine": "pdf"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Consider the stochastic optimization problem:",
          "confidence": 0.95,
          "engine": "pdf"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "min_{x ∈ ℝ^d} f(x) := E_{ξ ∼ D}[F(x; ξ)]",
          "confidence": 0.88,
          "engine": "formula_ocr",
          "metadata": {
            "latex": "\\min_{x \\in \\mathbb{R}^d} f(x) := \\mathbb{E}_{\\xi \\sim \\mathcal{D}}[F(x; \\xi)]",
            "label": "eq:problem"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "where F(x; ξ) is a stochastic component function, and we only have access to a noisy gradient oracle:",
          "confidence": 0.93,
          "engine": "pdf"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "g_k = ∇F(x_k; ξ_k) + ε_k,   E[ε_k] = 0,   E[∥ε_k∥²] ≤ σ²",
          "confidence": 0.85,
          "engine": "formula_ocr",
          "metadata": {
            "latex": "g_k = \\nabla F(x_k; \\xi_k) + \\varepsilon_k, \\quad \\mathbb{E}[\\varepsilon_k] = 0, \\quad \\mathbb{E}[\\|\\varepsilon_k\\|^2] \\leq \\sigma^2",
            "label": "eq:gradient_oracle"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "number": 2,
